      "role": "user",
      "content": "I got about −R22 988.32. Does the minus mean the yearly decrease should be written as a negative amount?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The slope and its negative sign are understood; the uncertainty is about reporting the decrease. Explain that the sign describes the downward trend, but the amount of the decrease is its positive magnitude. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"abs((-9839/428)*1000)\") -> 22988.317757009346]",
      "content": "The negative sign shows that the selling price goes down as the car gets older. Since the question asks for the **amount of the decrease**, give the positive size of the slope.\n\n**Estimated average yearly decrease: R22 988.32 per year.**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $b_1=\\dfrac{\\sum (x-\\bar{x})(y-\\bar{y})}{\\sum (x-\\bar{x})^2}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $x$: the car’s age in years.\n  * $y$: the car’s selling price in rands; $b_1$ is the regression slope.\n* **Why we use it:** The slope estimates how much the selling price changes for each extra year of age. Here it is about $-\\text{R}22\\,988.32$ per year, so the price decreases by about $\\text{R}22\\,988.32$ each year.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Keep the negative sign when stating the slope, but write a decrease as a positive amount when the question asks how much the price decreases."
